# PR lifecycle

- Open PRs as drafts while implementation, verification or requested revisions remain.
- If a reviewer requests changes, return the PR to draft before continuing repairs.
  Preserve the review and address its findings; do not dismiss it to clear status.
- Mark ready for review only after the agreed scope is implemented, relevant local
  checks pass, required visual evidence is current and known review findings are
  addressed. Respect an explicit user instruction to keep a PR draft.
- CI passing alone does not establish product quality or review readiness.
- Keep PR descriptions brief and current: outcome, useful visuals, concise changes,
  validation and material limitations. Identify pending checks honestly.
- Separate durable guidance changes from product implementation in independent PRs
  when requested. Avoid making a documentation PR depend on unrelated feature code.
- For our own PRs, obtain Jack's approval before merging into main unless the user
  explicitly authorizes a different independent reviewer. Never self-approve.
- When reviewing Jack's PRs, the user's standing direction is to fix actionable
  findings, approve when ready, and merge once all repository requirements pass.
  Do not stop at an approval when the PR is eligible to merge.
- Check that approval covers the current scope and head. Material changes require
  another review; do not dismiss change requests merely to clear merge status.
- Use squash merge by default, with a concise title and body describing the final
  change. Use a merge commit or rebase only when explicitly requested or when an
  agreed stack/history plan requires it; explain that choice in the PR.
- Merge the reviewed head explicitly (for example, `gh pr merge N --squash
  --match-head-commit SHA`). Never bypass required checks or review protections.
  If checks are pending, use auto-merge when available; otherwise report the exact
  blocker and resume the merge when it clears. Do not change repository settings
  merely to make a blocked merge pass.
- Approval and merging do not authorize release publication, tagging, deployment
  or deletion of another contributor's branch. Follow the task's explicit scope.
